D={
"Neanderthal extinction":"Neanderthals had lived in Europe and western Asia for over 300,000 years, burying their dead, making jewellery from eagle talons and possibly painting caves. They vanished around 40,000 years ago, a few thousand years after Homo sapiens spread into Europe. Likely causes combine small, inbred populations, abrupt climate swings, competition with modern humans for game, and absorption through interbreeding — so part of them lives on in our DNA.",
"Chauvet Cave paintings":"Discovered in 1994 in the Ardèche gorge of France, Chauvet holds over 400 animal images made around 36,000–30,000 years ago: lions, rhinos, cave bears, mammoths and horses, drawn with shading and overlapping figures to suggest movement. A cave bear skull placed on a rock and footprints of a child show human activity. Its age proved that sophisticated art appeared early, not gradually.",
"Dog domestication":"Dogs descend from a now-extinct population of grey wolves and were domesticated by Ice Age hunter-gatherers, at least 15,000 and possibly over 20,000 years ago. A dog was buried with humans at Bonn-Oberkassel about 14,000 years ago. Dogs helped hunting, guarding and hauling, and ancient DNA shows they travelled with human migrations, including into the Americas.",
"White Sands footprints":"Footprints of adults and children preserved in ancient lake beds at White Sands, New Mexico, were dated by seeds and pollen to 21,000–23,000 years ago. They show people living in North America during the Last Glacial Maximum, earlier than the old 'Clovis first' model. The prints also record mammoths and giant sloths walking alongside.",
"Clovis culture":"From about 13,000 years ago, people across North America made distinctive fluted 'Clovis' points, found with mammoth and mastodon kills. The Anzick child burial in Montana (12,600 years ago) is genetically ancestral to many Native Americans. Clovis lasted only a few centuries as megafauna disappeared.",
"Jericho walls and tower":"Around 8000 BCE the Neolithic town of Jericho in the Jordan valley, supported by a spring, had perhaps 1,000–2,000 people. They built a stone wall and an 8.5-metre tower with an internal staircase — among the oldest monumental buildings. Later residents plastered and painted the skulls of ancestors, a form of ancestor veneration.",
"Rice cultivation in the Yangtze":"Wild rice was gathered in the Yangtze valley from about 10,000 years ago; by 7,000 years ago, at Hemudu and Tianluoshan, people grew domesticated rice in paddies, kept pigs and built wooden stilt houses. Rice farming spread south into Southeast Asia and, mixing with locally cultivated rice, into India. Rice supports more people per hectare than most crops, enabling dense populations in Asia.",
"Maize domestication":"About 9,000 years ago farmers in the Balsas river valley of southern Mexico began selecting teosinte, a grass with tiny hard kernels. Over thousands of years, maize became a large-cobbed crop that could not seed itself without humans. Combined with beans and squash (the 'Three Sisters'), it fed the Olmec, Maya, Aztec and Puebloan worlds, and after 1492 it spread across Africa, India and China.",
"Copper smelting":"At Belovode in Serbia, people smelted copper around 5000 BCE; Iran and Anatolia show early metallurgy too. Smelting required furnaces reaching 1,100 °C. Copper tools and ornaments marked the Chalcolithic, and mixing copper with tin or arsenic made bronze, harder and better for weapons, around 3300 BCE.",
"Horse domestication":"People at Botai in Kazakhstan used horses around 3500 BCE, but ancient DNA (2021) shows the horses we ride today came from the lower Volga–Don steppe about 2200 BCE. They spread across Eurasia within centuries along with spoke-wheeled chariots, transforming warfare, trade and migration.",
"Wheel and wagon":"The earliest wheels, for pottery, appear in Mesopotamia around 3500 BCE; soon after, solid wooden wheels on axles carried wagons, depicted on the Bronocice pot in Poland (about 3400 BCE). Ox-drawn wagons let steppe peoples move herds and homes. Spoked wheels (about 2000 BCE) made fast chariots possible.",
"Narmer unifies Egypt":"Around 3100 BCE Narmer, a king from Upper Egypt, brought the Nile valley and delta under one rule. The Narmer Palette, found at Hierakonpolis, shows him wearing the white crown of the south on one side and the red crown of the north on the other. He founded the First Dynasty, beginning 3,000 years of pharaonic history.",
"Uruk, the first city":"By 3200 BCE Uruk covered 2.5 square km with perhaps 40,000 people, monumental temples such as the White Temple of Anu, and specialised craftsmen. Its administrators invented proto-cuneiform writing to track grain, beer and labour. Its expansion spread Mesopotamian culture across the Near East; later Gilgamesh was remembered as its king.",
"Sargon founds Akkad":"About 2334 BCE Sargon, a cupbearer to the king of Kish, seized power, built a new capital at Akkad, and conquered the Sumerian cities, creating the first multi-ethnic empire. He kept a standing army and appointed Akkadians as governors. His grandson Naram-Sin called himself 'king of the four quarters'.",
"Indus mature urban phase":"From about 2600 to 1900 BCE, the Indus or Harappan civilisation had over 1,000 settlements across 1 million square km. Cities like Mohenjo-daro, Harappa, Dholavira and Rakhigarhi had planned streets, covered drains, standard bricks and weights, and the Great Bath. They traded with Mesopotamia ('Meluhha'). Weakening monsoons and shifting rivers led to de-urbanisation.",
"Code of Hammurabi":"Around 1754 BCE King Hammurabi of Babylon placed 282 laws on a basalt stele, topped with an image of him receiving authority from Shamash. Laws covered trade, wages, divorce and medicine, with punishments that depended on class. The stele was taken to Susa and found there in 1901.",
"Hyksos expelled from Egypt":"The Hyksos, West Asian rulers who introduced horses, chariots and composite bows, controlled northern Egypt from about 1650 BCE. Kings of Thebes fought back; Ahmose I captured their capital Avaris around 1550 BCE. Egypt then built an empire in the Levant and Nubia, the New Kingdom.",
"Akhenaten's religious revolution":"Amenhotep IV renamed himself Akhenaten, promoting the Aten, the sun disc, as Egypt's supreme god, closing other temples and building a new capital, Amarna. Art became naturalistic. After his death, the old gods returned under Tutankhamun, and Akhenaten's name was erased.",
"Trojan War (traditional)":"Homer's Iliad tells of Greeks besieging Troy to recover Helen. Excavations at Hisarlik in Turkey by Schliemann and later teams found a city destroyed around 1180 BCE. Hittite records mention 'Wilusa', likely Troy. Whether the war happened as told is debated.",
"Iron smelting spreads":"Iron requires hotter furnaces and forging but iron ore is more common than tin. After the Bronze Age collapse disrupted tin trade around 1200 BCE, iron spread across the Near East and Mediterranean. In India, iron appears at sites in the Ganga valley and south India by 1800–1500 BCE; in Africa, Nok and Meroë became iron centres.",
"Rigveda composed":"Between about 1500 and 1200 BCE, poet-priests in the Punjab composed over 1,000 hymns to Agni, Indra, Soma and others. They were memorised exactly using techniques such as pada and ghana recitation, and written down only around 1000 CE. UNESCO lists Vedic chanting as intangible heritage.",
"Olympic Games founded":"In 776 BCE, according to tradition, the first Olympic Games were held at Olympia in honour of Zeus, with a single footrace. Held every four years, they grew to include wrestling, chariot races and the pankration; a sacred truce allowed athletes to travel. Theodosius I may have ended them around 393 CE; Pierre de Coubertin revived them in 1896.",
"Rome founded (legend)":"Roman legend says Romulus and Remus, sons of Mars, were suckled by a she-wolf and founded Rome on 21 April 753 BCE. Archaeology shows Iron Age huts on the Palatine from around 1000 BCE. The legend shaped Roman identity.",
"Fall of Nineveh":"In 612 BCE a coalition of Babylonians and Medes sacked Nineveh, the Assyrian capital. Assyria, once the greatest empire, collapsed by 609 BCE. Ashurbanipal's library was buried, preserving thousands of tablets.",
"Babylonian captivity":"Nebuchadnezzar II destroyed Jerusalem and the First Temple in 587 BCE and deported Judah's elite to Babylon. In exile, Jewish scholars began compiling the Hebrew Bible. Cyrus allowed their return in 538 BCE.",
"Cyrus takes Babylon":"In 539 BCE Cyrus captured Babylon with little fighting, presenting himself as chosen by Marduk. The Cyrus Cylinder records his restoring of temples and deportees.",
"Athenian democracy":"In 508 BCE Cleisthenes reorganised Athens into ten tribes and gave power to the assembly of male citizens. Officials were chosen by lot, and ostracism could exile dangerous men. Women, slaves and foreigners were excluded.",
"Battle of Marathon":"In 490 BCE about 10,000 Athenians and Plataeans defeated a larger Persian force at Marathon. Legend says a runner carried the news to Athens.",
"Peloponnesian War":"Athens and Sparta fought from 431 to 404 BCE; plague killed Pericles, and Athens' Sicilian expedition was destroyed. Sparta won with Persian money.",
"Death of Socrates":"In 399 BCE an Athenian jury condemned Socrates to death for impiety and corrupting youth; he drank hemlock, as described by Plato.",
"Alexander conquers Persia":"Alexander defeated Darius III at the Granicus (334), Issus (333) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), burned Persepolis and took the empire.",
"Chandragupta–Seleucus treaty":"About 303 BCE Seleucus ceded the northwest to Chandragupta for 500 elephants; Megasthenes went to Pataliputra as ambassador and wrote the Indica.",
}
